## 6. Mac Internet Troubleshooting

Initial symptoms:

```text
Mac → 172.16.10.1       PASS
Mac → 192.168.8.1       PASS
Mac → 8.8.8.8           FAIL
DNS resolution          PASS
HTTPS Internet access   FAIL
```

Potential causes investigated included:

- macOS service order;
- static routing;
- macOS Packet Filter;
- macOS Firewall;
- Stealth Mode;
- MikroTik forwarding;
- MikroTik NAT;
- MikroTik firewall.

### macOS Routing

macOS correctly selected:

```text
Destination: 8.8.8.8
Gateway:     172.16.10.1
Interface:   en0
```

### Packet Capture

RouterOS packet capture confirmed packets from:

```text
172.16.10.25
```

were received on the CyberTron LAN.

A capture on `ether1` then confirmed source NAT:

```text
172.16.10.25
      ↓
192.168.8.151 → 8.8.8.8
```

Therefore:

- Mac routing was correct;
- CYB-RTR-01 forwarding was correct;
- source NAT was correct;
- packets were leaving the MikroTik WAN.

### Root Cause

The Skinny mobile-data allowance had been exhausted.

The upstream provider presented its out-of-credit/data page.

The actual fault therefore existed beyond CYB-RTR-01.

### Lesson

Do not assume a failed Internet test proves a local routing/firewall fault.

The investigation progressively validated:

1. host addressing;
2. local gateway;
3. upstream gateway;
4. routing;
5. DNS;
6. packet arrival;
7. firewall forwarding;
8. NAT;
9. WAN transmission;
10. upstream service availability.

This prevented unnecessary changes to working security controls.

